#!/bin/sh
cat <<'MSG'

validity-fprintd is installed but not yet running.

It serves the same D-Bus name as fprintd (net.reactivated.Fprint), so the two
cannot run together. If fprintd is installed, stop and disable it first:

  rc-service fprintd stop
  rc-update del fprintd default

Then start this one:

  rc-update add validity-fprintd default
  rc-service validity-fprintd start

Check that your sensor is recognised:

  rc-service validity-fprintd stop
  validity-provision
  rc-service validity-fprintd start

If it reports unformatted flash or an unpaired sensor, see "Provisioning a bare
sensor" in /usr/share/doc/validity-fprintd/README.md. A sensor that has been
used with Windows Hello is already provisioned.

For fingerprint login, install fprintd-pam and reference pam_fprintd from
/etc/pam.d, keeping it "sufficient" so a failed finger falls through to the
password.

MSG
exit 0
